Adds a BWT mode that also gives the row of one suffix, so that bz2-ng's
sorter can take the BWT from libsais's final induction instead of reading
it out of the suffix array afterwards.

libsais's BWT mode (LIBSAIS_FLAGS_BWT) writes each row's preceding byte
into the row's own entry as the final induction passes it, but returns
only the row of suffix 0; libsais_bwt_aux adds the rows of every r-th
suffix, in an array beside the work area that bz2-ng's work array has no
room for. bz2-ng needs the row of one suffix, origPtr's. Under the new
flag LIBSAIS_FLAGS_BWT_ROW, libsais_induce_final_order_8u_omp runs
copies of the two BWT scans that note the row into which suffix r is
induced and store it in I[0], reusing the r and I that libsais_main_8u
already passes down for the aux mode. Every suffix but n - 1 is induced
into its final row exactly once, an L-type one in the left-to-right scan
and an S-type one in the right-to-left scan; n - 1 is induced before the
first scan and is checked there. The copies run single-threaded, as
bz2-ng always does; the OpenMP paths are untouched.

The mode uses the same work array as the suffix-array mode: the only
difference from it is in the final induction, which in both modes reads
the block and writes the suffix array's own entries and the context's
bucket counters, and nothing else.

The sorter then narrows the entries straight into L, where it used to
read the byte before every suffix out of the suffix array (a random
access per row) into the free space and copy it to L.

Measured at level 9 against an identical-binary control, 5 rounds in
rotating order, bz2-ng's encoder with and without this patch: Graviton4
with gcc 13 and clang 18 compresses MC:Z streams of 16 and 64 KiB and the
MC:Z, XA:Z, quality-string and Silesia whole files 1.02 times faster;
Granite Rapids is even with gcc 13 and 1.02 on the MC:Z and quality whole
files with clang 18; Zen 5 is even with both; the M4 with Apple clang 21
(screening, no control) 1.01-1.03. No cell is slower. Decompression is
unchanged.

